From 217318470182132d47f283ca2dd03e2270296222 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.local>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 06:17:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Assistant: understand full messages, explain failures safely,
 fix plan UX

Three separate reported problems, all in the guided-purchase assistant
(major_ai_assistant_screen.dart on Flutter, MajorAssistant.tsx on web) -
fixed on both platforms so they stay in parity, plus one small backend
addition both call.

1) Flutter's plan-selection step had NO buttons at all (every other step
   does) - the customer was expected to correctly type a plan's exact size
   from a plain text list, with a fragile substring-match underneath. This
   is what 'stuck at picking the data button' was - fixed by adding
   options (matching how the web version already worked), sorting plans
   cheapest-first, and layering exact-match -> loose-text-match -> single-
   plan-auto-select the same way web already does.

2) Plan validity was missing from BOTH platforms (web has the same gap,
   not just Flutter) even though the backend has always returned it -
   added to the plan buttons and the review/confirmation summary.

3) 'Very smart' requests, addressed two ways:
   - Superlative recognition ('cheapest'/'simplest'/'mafi rahusa'/'sauki')
     at the plan step auto-picks the (now cheapest-first-sorted) first
     plan directly, on both platforms - this is the literal 'MTN 1GB,
     which is simplest' example from the report.
   - Flutter gained _tryRichAnswer(): mid-flow, if a message plausibly
     carries more than the single value the current step is narrowly
     waiting for (an 11-digit phone number, or a size like '1GB'), the
     full NLU parse used on the very first message runs again and the
     conversation jumps to the furthest step the combined information
     resolves, instead of making the customer answer one field at a time.
     Guarded to only fire on messages that actually look richer than a
     one-word answer, so a bare 'MTN' or '500' still resolves instantly
     with no extra network round trip.
   - 'Why did my transaction fail?' is answered directly, from any step,
     on both platforms, via a new safe backend endpoint (below) - never a
     raw technical/provider error.

Also fixed along the way (same bug class as the earlier backend intent-
parser fix, just found here too):
   - Flutter's _parseNetwork() treated a bare digit '9' anywhere in the
     message as '9mobile' - a phone number or amount containing a '9'
     could misdetect the network. Same bug existed on web.
   - Both platforms' data-type detection ('corporate'/'share'/'gift'/
     'sme') used unbounded .includes(), same false-positive risk as the
     intent parser bug already fixed server-side (e.g. 'corporate' could
     misfire inside 'incorporated'). Now word-boundary safe on both.

Backend: GET /api/assistant/last-transaction (assistant.routes.ts) -
returns only type/status/amount/description/created_at for the customer's
most recent transaction. Deliberately does NOT return metadata or
providerRef (both present on the raw row and in GET /transactions) -
those can carry upstream provider payloads and internal error detail that
means nothing to a customer and shouldn't reach one. The client builds the
actual bilingual sentence from this, same pattern as every other assistant
response.

Verified against the actual current repo state (not a stale local clone)
before committing - diffed all 4 touched files against a fresh clone of
this same HEAD first to confirm nothing recently added elsewhere
(delivery tracking / NIN modification endpoints in app_endpoints.dart)
was getting overwritten. Backend: 155/155 tests passing. Web: tsc + vite
build clean.
